 was  not the cause  of freddie gray's death but rather the  effect what do you mean  yeah what what what i mean is uh the the  history of of inequitable policing  is is as old as as this nation's history  i mean when we're talking about  uh you know the reality is is that uh  the sound of  police sirens uh it has a different  pitch  depending on what neighborhood that  you're in but when we're looking at what  happened to  freddie it's important that we deal with  the issues that caused his  his his untimely death it's important  that we deal with the issues of policing  reform it's important that we issue  that we deal with the issues of being  able to address what happened in his  death  but what i also mean it's important that  we understand what happened in his life  we have to understand the conditions  that existed  when you had a young man who was born  premature  underweight addicted to heroin  moved into a home after he gained enough  weight to  to to be able to leave the hospital his  mother never made it to high school  and then moved into a home that in 2009  that home  and 400 other homes were named  in a lawsuit because of the endemic  levels of lead  inside of this home so we now had a  young man who was born underweight  addicted to heroin lead poisoned and by  this time in his life he is two years  old  and so what i mean by that is we have to  deal  with the issue of policing but we also  have to understand  that it's not just enough to mourn his  death  we've got to mourn his life and we've  got to be able to put together systems  and structures  that can make sure that young men like  freddie actually have a chance  in our large society yeah you write that  that uh  in baltimore the odds started being  stacked against freddie  generations before he was born when you  look at what's happening right now wes  do you think something really is  different this time because as you say  we've watched this video so many times  now in the last five years  i think something is different and i  think the difference is is that the bar  is being raised  uh the bars being raised as the what we  identify as progress  you know one common misperception i  think about what happened in  in baltimore five years ago is there's  this idea that what what calmed  everything down  was when the national guard was called  in that's not true  the protests that were actually planned  on the saturday after  the uprising that took place in  baltimore were said to be as large  you know or larger than any of the  protests that we had seen thus far  what calmed everything down was when the  state's attorney actually decided to  press charges  against the six officers so there was  almost this euphoric feeling  in baltimore that justice might actually  be here  we actually might have people who are  accountable for the death  of of an unarmed black man that was able  to calm everything down  there was a consent decree that was put  inside of baltimore where the department  of justice  very clearly laid out the patterns and  practices  of systemic racism and injustice that  the police department was having on  black communities  here's the difference years later  no one was convicted for what happened  to freddie gray  a consent decree was rolled back and so  i think the bar is now risen  now what we're talking about right now  is not just simply about charges being  filed and consent decrees being filed  what we're talking about now  are laws being changed what we're  talking about now is  justice in every single form not just  justice in terms of policing  but justice in terms of economics  justice in terms of health so the bar is  now being risen  as to what progress is and i think  people are being very very clear about  that  yeah we should remind people that all of  the officers involved in  gray's case were either acquitted or had  the charges dropped against them  the baltimore police department told us  we continue to make  significant progress in implementing  comprehensive reforms  have they gone far enough wes  i i don't think that they would argue  that they've gone far enough yet uh  you know i think we we continue to watch  uh individual and smaller movements on  something that's a larger systemic  problem you know it's difficult for me  to have a conversation about good apples  versus bad apples when we have to reform  systems  um you know good apples and bad apples  don't exist when you still have systems  that need to be uprooted and i think  what ends up happening is you know we  have a lot of people who are  coming from a place of good will in this  we have a lot of people who are coming  from  you know a place a place of of of  sympathy and empathy towards what we're  seeing  but we cannot confuse or or or in any  way mistake  good deeds as being enough to deal with  larger injustices and it's unfair  it wasn't more than one of the reasons  why i wanted to follow these eight  characters  wes okay wes moore thank you so much for  being with us the book again is five  days it's on sale  june 23rd my birthday buy it we'll be  right back
 

